title: 'MILESTONE: M2.5 merge-by-meaning core works and the fail-closed falsifier PASSES — the multi-branch capability git can''t do'
summary: 'The core of M2.5 branching — the founder''s readiness bar (work N durable branches at once, merged by meaning) — is built and the acceptance gate PASSES. Four increments committed: foundations + DAG-reachable grading (af9c2bce), branch/switch verbs (fe7f5219), the weaveTips spine refactor (40ec9139, behavior-preserving), and merge-by-meaning with the fail-closed rule (a7e9b12e). Jinx''s divergent-branch falsifier passes independently: the two canonical §8 breaks (config×code, no-shared-token invariant const LIMIT 100→50) surface a HELD naming the byte-decided path instead of a silent CLEAN, the control auto-folds, same-symbol KNOTs. So merge is SAFER than git''s silent auto-merge, not a blind-class multiplier — which was the advocate''s whole objection. Full suite 1133 passing, admit''s linear path byte-identical. Remaining (hardening/exposure, not core): protected main + merge-to-protected-human-only, lock-narrowing for the per-branch-CAS concurrency win, multi-branch log/diff, and the MCP/daemon skins.'
