body: |-
  THE GATE: TD-2026-08-12-813 made Jinx's divergent-branch merge falsifier the non-negotiable acceptance test. It passes (test/merge-falsifier.test.ts, verified independently): (a) config×code .js value → HELD; (b) no-shared-token invariant (scalar const not lifted → byte-decided) → HELD naming the file; (c) control disjoint lifted functions → auto-fold CLEAN (value prop survives); (d) same-symbol → KNOT. The fail-closed rule: on a merge, classifyMergePaths runs BEFORE any ref moves; any byte-decided change (meaning was blind) → HELD requiring human --confirm, ref not advanced. Admit never sets the flag → linear behavior unchanged.

  WHY THIS ANSWERS THE DISSENT: Jinx showed merge-by-meaning's CLEAN trigger is the inverse of git's conflict trigger, so naive merge would auto-fold the §8 blind classes git surfaces. The fail-closed rule inverts that: meaning auto-folds only what it SAW; anything it was blind to is held for a human. Git silently auto-merges disjoint config×code; Warpline holds it. That is 'better than git at the merge' made true and honest.

  BUILD SHAPE (Arky was right): ~cheap wiring on a finished engine. merge = weaveTips (the factored admit core) pointed at two branch tips with base=LCA. The only net-new algorithm was the merge-base. Every increment verified (tsc + full suite) before commit; the spine refactor changed ZERO existing tests.

  REMAINING increments (5-7): protected main + merge-to-protected=HUMAN_ONLY (Aegis's anti-self-merge gate — a swarm must not auto-land into main); lock-narrowing to unlock the per-branch-CAS concurrency win (Aegis — risky: fabric.jsonl append isn't atomic, C-13); multi-branch log + diff A..B; MCP/daemon/descriptor skins for branch/switch/merge (F4 legibility). Plus follow-ups: cross-branch clean switch (expectTreeId baseline), dedicated meaning-blind RefusalCode.

  ARC COMMITS: ...58cb76e9 (pre-fixes) → judge → Tier-1 dogfood (36bd7c73, 964f047c) → multi-instance (1b2f874a) → M2.5 (af9c2bce, fe7f5219, 40ec9139, a7e9b12e). All on warpline-surfaces at origin.
